.TH FOLD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fold \- wrap each input line to fit a specified width
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fold
.RB [ \-bs ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width " |"
.BI \- width ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fold
copies each
.I file
(or standard input if no
.I file
is given) to standard output, inserting a newline wherever a line
would otherwise exceed
.I width
columns.
A
.I file
of
.B \-
means standard input.
.PP
By default the width is 80 and positions are counted in display
columns:
a tab advances to the next multiple of 8,
a backspace moves back one column (never below 0),
a carriage return resets the column to 0,
and every other byte occupies one column.
A newline in the input ends the line and resets the count.
A break is inserted before the character that would carry the line
past
.IR width ,
except that a line is never broken before its first character.
.PP
If the last line of the input has no terminating newline,
none is added.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes
Count bytes rather than columns: tabs, backspaces and carriage
returns each count as one position.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-spaces
Break after the last space or tab within the current line segment
instead of at the exact width.
If the segment contains no blank, the line is broken at the width.
.TP
.BR \-w " \fIwidth\fR, " \-\-width= \fIwidth\fR
Use
.I width
columns instead of 80.
The value is converted with
.BR atoi (3);
it must come out as a positive integer.
.TP
.BI \- width
Obsolete form of
.BR \-w .
Any argument before
.B \-\-
that consists of a
.B \-
followed only by digits is taken as the width.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage line to standard output and exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit with status 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was given, the width was not a positive integer,
or a
.I file
could not be opened.
Remaining files are still processed after an open failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wrap a file at 40 columns, breaking at blanks:
.PP
.EX
$ fold \-s \-w 40 notes.txt
.EE
.PP
Wrap standard input at 72 bytes:
.PP
.EX
$ cat log | fold \-b \-72
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pr (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR wc (1)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.B \-w
options follow POSIX.1-2024.
The long options follow GNU coreutils.
.SH BUGS
The width argument is parsed with
.BR atoi (3),
so trailing garbage (for example
.BR "\-w 10x" )
is silently ignored.
Read errors on input are not reported.
